mi","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2507-8619"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Pharmaceutical Science, Tohoku University, Sendai, Miyagi 980-8578, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Inoue A","firstName":"Asuka","lastName":"Inoue","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Pharmaceutical Science, Tohoku University, Sendai, Miyagi 980-8578, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Japan Science and Technology Agency (JST), Precursory Research for Embryonic Science and Technology (PRESTO), Kawaguchi, Saitama 332-0012, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Aoki J","firstName":"Junken","lastName":"Aoki","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9435-1896"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Pharmaceutical Science, Tohoku University, Sendai, Miyagi 980-8578, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Core Research for Evolutionary Science and Technology (AMED-CREST), Japan Agency for Medical Research and Development, Chiyoda-ku, Tokyo 100-0004, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Steyaert J","firstName":"Jan","lastName":"Steyaert","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3825-874X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Structural Biology Brussels, Vrije Universiteit Brussels, B-1050 Brussels, Belgium."},{"affiliation":"Structural Biology Research Center, Vlaams Instituut voor Biotechnologie, B-1050 Brussels, Belgium."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun JP","firstName":"Jin-Peng","lastName":"Sun","initials":"JP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Biology and Biochemistry, Shandong University School of Medicine, Jinan, Shandong 250012, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Bouvier M","firstName":"Michel","lastName":"Bouvier","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1128-0100"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Institute for Research in Immunology and Cancer, University of Montreal, Montreal, QC H3C 3J7, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Skiniotis G","firstName":"Georgios","lastName":"Skiniotis","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0238-7846"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Life Sciences Institute, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI 48109."}]}},{"fullName":"Lefkowitz RJ","firstName":"Robert J","lastName":"Lefkowitz","initials":"RJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Duke University Medical Center, Durham, NC 27710; lefko001@receptor-biol.duke.edu."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Duke University Medical Center, Durham, NC 27710."},{"affiliation":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Duke University Medical Center, Durham, NC 27710."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9435-1896"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2507-8619"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3169-8873"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3825-874X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5189-5647"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6079-3992"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8321-0796"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0238-7846"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1128-0100"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1638-8911"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2034-2684"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2910-8288"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-8904-9972"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-3916-1872"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["wikipedia","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"114","journalIssueId":2534003,"dateOfPublication":"2017 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2017,"printPublicationDate":"2017-03-01","journal":{"title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424","nlmid":"7505876"}},"pubYear":"2017","pageInfo":"2562-2567","abstractText":"β-Arrestins (βarrs) interact with G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) to desensitize G protein signaling, to initiate signaling on their own, and to mediate receptor endocytosis. Prior structural studies have revealed two unique conformations of GPCR-βarr complexes: the \"tail\" conformation, with βarr primarily coupled to the phosphorylated GPCR C-terminal tail, and the \"core\" conformation, where, in addition to the phosphorylated C-terminal tail, βarr is further engaged with the receptor transmembrane core. However, the relationship of these distinct conformations to the various functions of βarrs is unknown. Here, we created a mutant form of βarr lacking the \"finger-loop\" region, which is unable to form the core conformation but retains the ability to form the tail conformation. We find that the tail conformation preserves the ability to mediate receptor internalization and βarr signaling but not desensitization of G protein signaling. Thus, the two GPCR-βarr conformations can carry out distinct functions.","affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Duke University Medical Center, Durham, NC 27710.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"NA","agency":"Gouvernement du Canada | Canadian Institutes of Health Research","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"NA","agency":"Lundbeckfonden","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 DK090165","agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"MOP10501","agency":"Gouvernement du Canada | Canadian Institutes of Health Research","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T32 HL007101","agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"5F30HL129803","agency":"HHS | NIH | National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 GM069905","agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 HL016037","agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T32 GM145449","agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T32HL007101","agency":"HHS | NIH | National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"NA","agency":"Det Frie Forskningsråd","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"NA","agency":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R172-2014-1468","agency":"Lundbeck Foundation","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","acronym":"HHMI","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"DK090165","agency":"HHS | NIH | National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"F30 HL129803","agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"S10 OD020011","agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"T32 GM007171","agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Multiprotein Complexes"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"GTP-Binding Protein Regulators","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Receptors, G-Protein-Coupled","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Endocytosis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Amino Acid Sequence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Molecular Conformation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mutant Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"HEK293 Cells"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"beta-Arrestins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Signaling","Endocytosis","Desensitization","Arrestin","Gpcr"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Multiprotein Complexes","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Receptors, G-Protein-Coupled","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"GTP-Binding Protein Regulators","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Mutant Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"beta-Arrestins","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://www.pnas.org/content/pnas/114/10/2562.full.pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1701529114"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC5347553"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC5347553?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1701529114"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":340,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2018-04-18","dateOfCreation":"2017-02-23","firstIndexDate":"2017-02-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-03","dateOfRevision":"2026-04-08","electronicPublicationDate":"2017-02-21","firstPublicationDate":"2017-02-21"},{"id":"39107078","source":"MED","pmid":"39107078","pmcid":"PMC11413913","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC11413913"]},"doi":"10.1124/molpharm.124.000974","title":"The Impact of Nanobodies on G Protein-Coupled Receptor Structural Biology and Their Potential as Therapeutic Agents.","authorString":"Salom D, Wu A, Liu CC, Palczewski K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Salom D","firstName":"David","lastName":"Salom","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Gavin Herbert Eye Institute - Center for Translational Vision Research, Department of Ophthalmology (D.S., A.W., K.P.) and Department of Biomedical Engineering (C.C.L.), University of California, Irvine, Irvine, California kpalczew@uci.edu dsalom1@hs.uci.edu arumh2@hs.uci.edu ccl@uci.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu A","firstName":"Arum","lastName":"Wu","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Gavin Herbert Eye Institute - Center for Translational Vision Research, Department of Ophthalmology (D.S., A.W., K.P.) and Department of Biomedical Engineering (C.C.L.), University of California, Irvine, Irvine, California kpalczew@uci.edu dsalom1@hs.uci.edu arumh2@hs.uci.edu ccl@uci.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu CC","firstName":"Chang C","lastName":"Liu","initials":"CC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Gavin Herbert Eye Institute - Center for Translational Vision Research, Department of Ophthalmology (D.S., A.W., K.P.) and Department of Biomedical Engineering (C.C.L.), University of California, Irvine, Irvine, California kpalczew@uci.edu dsalom1@hs.uci.edu arumh2@hs.uci.edu ccl@uci.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Palczewski K","firstName":"Krzysztof","lastName":"Palczewski","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Gavin Herbert Eye Institute - Center for Translational Vision Research, Department of Ophthalmology (D.S., A.W., K.P.) and Department of Biomedical Engineering (C.C.L.), University of California, Irvine, Irvine, California kpalczew@uci.edu dsalom1@hs.uci.edu arumh2@hs.uci.edu ccl@uci.edu."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"106","journalIssueId":3829984,"dateOfPublication":"2024 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2024,"printPublicationDate":"2024-09-01","journal":{"title":"Molecular pharmacology","medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Pharmacol","isoabbreviation":"Mol Pharmacol","essn":"1521-0111","issn":"0026-895X","nlmid":"0035623"}},"pubYear":"2024","pageInfo":"155-163","abstractText":"The family of human G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) comprises about 800 different members, with about 35% of current pharmaceutical drugs targeting GPCRs. However, GPCR structural biology, necessary for structure-guided drug design, has lagged behind that of other membrane proteins, and it was not until the year 2000 when the first crystal structure of a GPCR (rhodopsin) was solved. Starting in 2007, the determination of additional GPCR structures was facilitated by protein engineering, new crystallization techniques, complexation with antibody fragments, and other strategies. More recently, the use of camelid heavy-chain-only antibody fragments (nanobodies) as crystallographic chaperones has revolutionized the field of GPCR structural biology, aiding in the determination of more than 340 GPCR structures to date. In most cases, the GPCR structures solved as complexes with nanobodies (Nbs) have revealed the binding mode of cognate or non-natural ligands; in a few cases, the same Nb has acted as an orthosteric or allosteric modulator of GPCR signaling. In this review, we summarize the multiple ingenious strategies that have been conceived and implemented in the last decade to capitalize on the discovery of nanobodies to study GPCRs from a structural perspective. SIGNIFICANCE STATEMENT: G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) are major pharmacological targets, and the determination of their structures at high resolution has been essential for structure-guided drug design and for insights about their functions. Single-domain antibodies (nanobodies) have greatly facilitated the structural determination of GPCRs by forming complexes directly with the receptors or indirectly through protein partners.","affiliation":"Gavin Herbert Eye Institute - Center for Translational Vision Research, Department of Ophthalmology (D.S., A.W., K.P.) and Department of Biomedical Engineering (C.C.L.), University of California, Irvine, Irvine, California kpalczew@uci.edu dsalom1@hs.uci.edu arumh2@hs.uci.edu ccl@uci.edu.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"R01 EY009339","agency":"NEI NIH HHS","acronym":"EY","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 EY034519","agency":"NEI NIH HHS","acronym":"EY","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"P30 EY034070","agency":"NEI NIH HHS","acronym":"EY","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Receptors, G-Protein-Coupled","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ligands"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein Conformation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Single-Domain Antibodies","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Ligands","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Receptors, G-Protein-Coupled","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Single-Domain Antibodies","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://molpharm.aspetjournals.org/content/molpharm/early/2024/08/06/molpharm.124.000974.full.pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1124/molpharm.124.000974"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC11413913"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC11413913?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1124/molpharm.124.000974"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":13,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["gen","pdb"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2024-09-17","dateOfCreation":"2024-08-06","firstIndexDate":"2024-08-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-10-03","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-21","electronicPublicationDate":"2024-09-17","firstPublicationDate":"2024-09-17","embargoDate":"2025-10-01"},{"id":"PPR800665","source":"PPR","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PPR800665"]},"doi":"10.1101/2024.02.01.578380","title":"Molecular basis of ligand promiscuity, structural mimicry, and atypical dimerization in the chemokine receptors","authorString":"Saha S, Sano FK, Sharma S, Ganguly M, Saha S, Akasaka H, Kobayashi T, Zaidi N, Mishra S, Dalal A, Mohapatra S, Yadav MK, Itoh Y, Leurs R, Chevigné A, Banerjee R, Shihoya W, Nureki O, Shukla AK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Saha S","firstName":"Shirsha","lastName":"Saha","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Sano FK","firstName":"Fumiya","lastName":"Sano","initials":"FK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Science, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan"}]}},{"fullName":"Sharma S","firstName":"Saloni","lastName":"Sharma","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Ganguly M","firstName":"Manisankar","lastName":"Ganguly","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Saha S","firstName":"Sayantan","lastName":"Saha","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Akasaka H","firstName":"Hiroaki","lastName":"Akasaka","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Science, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan"}]}},{"fullName":"Kobayashi T","firstName":"Takaaki","lastName":"Kobayashi","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Science, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan"}]}},{"fullName":"Zaidi N","firstName":"Nashrah","lastName":"Zaidi","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Mishra S","firstName":"Sudha","lastName":"Mishra","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Dalal A","firstName":"Annu","lastName":"Dalal","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-1762-7339"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Mohapatra S","firstName":"Samanwita","lastName":"Mohapatra","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Yadav MK","firstName":"Manish","lastName":"Yadav","initials":"MK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4297-7779"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Itoh Y","firstName":"Yuzuru","lastName":"Itoh","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7802-5572"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Science, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan"}]}},{"fullName":"Leurs R","firstName":"Rob","lastName":"Leurs","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Amsterdam Institute for Molecules, Medicines, and Systems (AIMMS), Division of Medicinal Chemistry, Faculty of Sciences, VU University Amsterdam, Amsterdam, The Netherlands"}]}},{"fullName":"Chevigné A","firstName":"Andy","lastName":"Chevigné","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Immuno-Pharmacology and Interactomics, Department of Infection and Immunity, Luxembourg Institute of Health, Esch-sur-Alzette, Luxembourg"}]}},{"fullName":"Banerjee R","firstName":"Ramanuj","lastName":"Banerjee","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7751-2317"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}},{"fullName":"Shihoya W","firstName":"Wataru","lastName":"Shihoya","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Science, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan"}]}},{"fullName":"Nureki O","firstName":"Osamu","lastName":"Nureki","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Science, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan"}]}},{"fullName":"Shukla AK","firstName":"Arun","lastName":"Shukla","initials":"AK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur, India"}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7802-5572"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7751-2317"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4297-7779"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-1762-7339"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"pubYear":"2024","abstractText":"Selectivity of natural agonists for their cognate receptors is one of the hallmarks of the members of GPCR family, and it is crucial for the specificity of downstream signal-transduction. However, this selectivity often breaks down in the chemokine receptor subfamily, wherein a high degree of promiscuity is observed with one receptor recognizing multiple chemokines and one chemokine binding to multiple receptors. The molecular determinants of such a striking promiscuity for natural ligands in the chemokine-chemokine receptor system remain mostly elusive and represent an important knowledge gap in our current understanding. Here, we carry out a comprehensive transducer-coupling analysis, testing all known C-X-C chemokines on every C-X-C type chemokine receptor, to generate a global fingerprint of the selectivity and promiscuity encoded within this system. Taking lead from our finding, we determined cryo-EM structures of the most promiscuous receptor, CXCR2, in complex with every interacting chemokine, and deciphered the conserved molecular signatures and distinct binding modalities. While most chemokines position themselves on the receptor as a dimer, CXCL6 exhibits a monomeric binding pose induced by a previously unanticipated reorientation of its carboxyl-terminal α-helix, leading to disruption of the dimer interface. Surprisingly, one of the chemokines, CXCL5, induces a ligand-swapped dimer of CXCR2, the first of its kind observed in class A GPCRs, wherein each protomer of the ligand engages its own receptor without any discernible receptor-receptor interface. These unique observations provide a possible structural mechanism for inherent functional specialization encoded in chemokines despite their convergence to a common receptor. Furthermore, we also determined cryo-EM structures of CXCR3 in complex with G-protein-biased and β-arrestin-biased small molecule agonists that elucidate distinct allosteric modulations in the receptor driving their divergent transducer-coupling bias. Guided by structural analysis and experimental validation, we discover that in contrast to previously held notion, small molecule agonists of CXCR3 display robust agonism at CXCR7, an intrinsically biased, β-arrestin-coupled receptor, making them first-in-class dual agonists for chemokine receptors with exclusive βarr-bias at CXCR7. Taken together, our study provides molecular insights into ligand promiscuity and signaling bias at the chemokine receptors, and also demonstrates a proof of principle that naturally encoded structural mimicry can be recapitulated using synthetic pharmacophores with potential implications for developing novel therapeutics.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"bioRxiv","yearOfPublication":2024},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/biorxiv/early/2024/02/02/2024.02.01.578380.full.pdf"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.02.01.578380"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/article/PPR/PPR800665"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/api/fulltextRepo?pprId=PPR800665&type=FILE&fileName=EMS193861-pdf.pdf&mimeType=application/pdf"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"Y","nihAuthMan":"N","manuscriptId":"EMS193861","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["pdb","doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2024-02-04","firstIndexDate":"2024-02-04","fullTextReceivedDate":"2024-02-12","firstPublicationDate":"2024-02-02"},{"id":"41461581","source":"MED","pmid":"41461581","pmcid":"PMC13242166","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13242166"]},"doi":"10.1111/bph.70230","title":"The Concise Guide to PHARMACOLOGY 2025/26: G protein-coupled receptors.","authorString":"Alexander SPH, Davenport AP, Kelly E, Gibb AJ, Mathie AA, Peach CJ, Veale EL, Armstrong JF, Fac